        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: mīror, mīrārī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mīror, mīrārī — \"to wonder, marvel at\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puer elephantum mīrātur. The boy marvels at the elephant."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Deponent: mīror, mīrārī, mīrātus sum. With the accusative, or with quod — I am surprised that. English miracle belongs to the same family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "mīror is built on mīrus \"wonderful\", which EtymDB traces through Proto-Italic to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to smile\". English admire and miracle belong to the same family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
