          "title": "You'll want to know: stupeō, stupēre",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stupeō, stupēre — \"to be stunned, be amazed\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Agricola in urbe magnā stupet. The farmer is stunned in the great city."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stupeō, stupēre, stupuī. Struck dumb with wonder or fear. English stupor comes from it, and stupid from its adjective stupidus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "stupeō is inherited and usually traced to a Proto-Indo-European root meaning \"to strike, to stun\" (EtymDB). English stupid comes from its adjective stupidus."
            }
          ]
        },
